# The specification's trust gate is implemented by four clients, in four different shapes

source: S198 — https://raw.githubusercontent.com/vinhnx/vtcode/main/docs/skills/SKILLS_GUIDE.md — the "Prompting Behavior" paragraph — retrieved 2026-10-24
source: S199 — https://parallelworks.com/docs/ai/code/skills — the "Trust" section — retrieved 2026-10-24
source: S200 — https://kilo.ai/docs/customize/skills — the "Trusted skills only" list — retrieved 2026-10-24
source: S201 — https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/agent-framework/agents/skills — the "Security best practices" subsection — retrieved 2026-10-24
source: S202 — https://raw.githubusercontent.com/mistralai/mistral-vibe/main/README.md — the "Skills System" section — retrieved 2026-10-24
verified-at: 2026-10-24
recheck: 90d — re-fetch all five and confirm each client's gate wording is unchanged (ran 2026-10-24)
ground: round-6 item 2, "Does any client implement the specification's trust gate?" — **Closed: four clients do, and they do not agree on what a gate is.**

**The lane's most important gap is closed, and it closes against a single answer.** The
client guide proposes that clients "gate project-level skill loading on a trust check",
so that an untrusted repository cannot silently inject instructions into the agent's
context. Round 6 recorded that after twenty client surfaces, the only evidence in the
lane was the specification paragraph itself plus one security paper. This round opens
four client surfaces that each document a gate, and they are four different mechanisms
rather than four instances of one.

Parallel Works is the closest to the specification's own proposal, and goes further.
Project skills "clear two independent gates": a workspace-level trust and a per-skill
execution grant issued with `/skills trust <name>`. The decisive sentence is that a
model "can never grant either gate" — the gate is not something the agent can talk its
way past. This is the only client in the lane whose gate is stated as a property of the
skill-loading path rather than of a command or a tool call.

Kilo Code gates execution rather than loading. A skill's commands run only when the
skill comes from a trusted location; a project skill and a skill fetched from a remote
URL never execute commands, and the skill body is replaced in place by a marker saying
it is untrusted. What the user then approves is a command list, not a skill: "every
command in the file is listed in a single permission prompt before anything runs".

Microsoft's Agent Framework expresses the same concern as approval by default on the
skill tools themselves — "All three skill tools require approval by default" — and adds
the input-side rule the lane had not seen stated by a client: MCP-sourced skill
responses are to be treated as untrusted input.

Mistral AI Vibe gates by filesystem trust, and is the only client of the four whose
trust boundary is not about skills at all. A project-level skill is discovered only when
its folder is trusted; the same annotation, "trusted folders only", appears on the
ordinary instruction files, so one trust decision governs both.

VT Code is the fourth surface and the weakest gate of the four, but it is the only one
that states the relationship between skill content and the host explicitly: skill
content "is untrusted resource data", and the host's own tool and sandbox policy is
authoritative over it. That is a posture rather than a mechanism, and the lane should
not count it as one.

What this changes for the lane is the shape of the remaining question. "Does any client
implement the trust gate?" was asked as if the answer were yes or no. The answer is
four yeses and one taxonomy: gate-at-load (Parallel Works, Vibe), gate-at-execute
(Kilo), gate-at-tool-call (Microsoft), declare-untrusted (VT Code). A client that
satisfies the specification's wording and a client that merely refuses to execute
untrusted commands are not the same thing, and the lane's own corpus now has to keep
them apart. The evidence also lands on the security side of the question rather than
against it: the spec's concern was silent injection, and the two clients that gate the
loading path are the two that close it most directly.


